Subject: Planning approach for a 7‑node transmission network connecting generators to consumers

We are planning a transmission network that links two generator nodes to five consumer nodes across a seven‑node footprint. The node locations are provided as map coordinates in {NODE_COORDINATES}, and the role of each node (generator or consumer) is identified in {NODE_TYPES}. Consumer power requirements are specified in {POWER_DEMANDS}, and generator output limits are defined in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}.

At a high level, our decisions center on which transmission lines to build between node pairs and how to route power across the built lines. Power may only flow along corridors we choose to construct. Construction expenditure is distance‑based at {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}, so longer corridors carry higher capital cost. Each built line will have a maximum permissible power flow of {LINE_CAPACITY}, and we must respect both line limits and generator capacity while ensuring every consumer’s demand is fully met.

We will use copper conductors. The wire material specification is provided in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the chosen conductor size is {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. These two parameters determine the resistance of each candidate line segment as a function of length: resistance rises with distance and falls with larger cross‑section. That resistance governs resistive losses (I^2R) when power is transmitted.

Operational costs are driven by the physical square‑law behavior of resistive heating. In our planning model, the loss cost on each line equals {LOSS_COST_RATE} times the I^2R losses, and those losses scale with the line resistance and the square of the power divided by the operating voltage {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}. In practical terms: if we double the power flow on a line, the resistive losses—and thus the heating cost—quadruple. Conversely, higher voltage reduces current for the same power and therefore cuts heating costs with a 1/voltage^2 effect. This square‑law behavior is central; we should not approximate losses as linear in flow or simply sum flows for cost. The physics of I^2R must be reflected in the cost model.

For planning purposes, we treat losses as a financial penalty rather than a physical deduction from delivered power. Nodal balances are enforced on a lossless basis: power entering a node equals power leaving it (plus local demand or minus local generation), and the network meets all consumer demands without subtracting losses from those balances. The resistive heating shows up as an external cost line item.

The objective is to minimize the total cost of the network: the sum of construction cost (distance times {BUILD_COST_PER_KM} for the set of lines we build) and the ongoing cost of resistive losses (the square‑law heating cost accumulated across all built lines). This creates an explicit trade‑off: building additional or longer corridors increases capital cost, but it can reduce operational expense by spreading power across more paths or shortening high‑flow routes, thereby lowering I^2R losses. Conversely, minimizing construction by relying on a few paths may look inexpensive at first, but high flows on those lines can drive up heating costs disproportionately.

Our deliverables will be a recommended set of lines to build and the corresponding power flows that:
- Meet all consumer demands in {POWER_DEMANDS}
- Respect generator limits in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}
- Stay within per‑line limits of {LINE_CAPACITY}
- Reflect copper conductor performance defined by {WIRE_RESISTIVITY} and {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}
- Account for square‑law resistive losses at {OPERATING_VOLTAGE} with loss costs at {LOSS_COST_RATE}
- Minimize the combined construction expense at {BUILD_COST_PER_KM} and the quadratic operational loss cost

This approach ensures the design captures the real physics of transmission losses and uses high voltage specifically to mitigate I^2R heating, while preserving clear, lossless power flow conservation at the nodes for planning and comparison of alternatives.
